      "obligation": "refuter:19:1",
      "id": "cor-cyclotomic-field-splits-a-finite-group",
      "location": "Proof 1.1–2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof begins, “$E\\supseteq F$ is a splitting extension and $V$ is an irreducible $E$-representation,” then asserts “Brauer induction expresses $[V]$” and later treats it as “one irreducible basis element of $R_{\\mathbb C}(G)$,” concluding $V\\cong W_i\\otimes_F\\mathbb C$. But the cited in-scope dependency thm-brauer-induction states only that “every complex virtual character” has the indicated expression. No map $F\\to\\mathbb C$ is assumed, and no scalar-change argument licenses replacing the $E$-representation $V$ by a complex one. Thus neither use of the cited theorem nor the displayed final isomorphism is well-typed under the stated hypotheses.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e4e931cf1241d7200570b8a0758e1b5b622543317eede2f9b5368402dc26077b"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:19:2",
      "id": "lem-elementary-detection-at-a-fixed-element",
      "location": "Proof 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 places $\\psi$ only in “the cyclotomic-coefficient elementary induction ideal,” while step 2.1 says “the preceding congruence gives the same conclusion at every $g$.” The cited in-scope lemma lem-p-primary-character-value-congruence has the explicit hypothesis “Let $\\chi\\in R(G)$ be integer-valued.” The proof never establishes $\\psi\\in R(G)$; integer-valued cyclotomic-coefficient character combinations need not be integral virtual characters. Therefore that lemma does not license the congruence for $\\psi$, on which the asserted $p^n$-divisibility depends.",
      "severity": "fatal",
